  "author_guidance": "In the 24-round test these actors stopped being able to do anything (or never could): dispatcher (refused: clear-passage: you are beside the brick wall: was 'hangar', needed 'airfield-office'; the brick wall passage is not already clear: was True, needed False, drink-water: you are with the glass of water: was 'hangar', needed 'airfield-office', move: you are at the passage's first place: was 'hangar', needed 'airfield-office'); instructor (refused: clear-passage: you are beside the brick wall: was 'hangar', needed 'airfield-office'; the brick wall passage is not already clear: was True, needed False, drink-water: you are with the glass of water: was 'hangar', needed 'airfield-office', move: you are at the passage's first place: was 'hangar', needed 'airfield-office'). Every actor role must be able to act regularly: make sure some actor's action brings what each one's action needs to where it is checked (for example, if a carrier must hold goods before delivering, give another actor an action that loads goods onto the carrier, or let the carrier pick them up itself). A process may renew raw materials at their source or bring new demand, but never supply what an action makes or move goods between places.",
  "authority_rules": [
    "Use only action participants as effect targets.",
    "A process acts on each matching entity `it`. When one physical change moves or changes a linked entity too (a driver riding in a vehicle, a hospital whose bed a dying patient frees), declare that entity in the process's participants (link owner_of_it, owned_by_it, co_located, or ref:<component>.<field>) and target it by that name in the same process; never split one physical change across two processes.",
    "When a consequence follows physically at once from another change (something unsupported falls, a fragile thing that fell breaks, a broken container spills, a breaking thing makes a sound), make it a process with timing immediately whose checks name the state that change leaves; each step of a chain is its own process. Keep timing each_round for what takes time (drying, growing, travelling, fuel).",
    "Checks on location/ownership/portable paths must agree with initial_builtin_state (null means unset), so that at least one action is possible from the starting state.",
    "Never check ownership.owner_ref or portable.portable on an entity whose initial_builtin_state value is null, unless one of your own effects sets that value first; use component fields instead.",
    "A participant's selector must list in `components` every component whose fields that participant's checks or effects read or write.",
    "When entities start in different places (initial_builtin_state location values differ), an action that uses, takes, operates or talks to a thing or person must check that the actor's location.location_id equals that participant's location.location_id.",
    "Every check must hold at the same time; there is no 'or'. Never require one value to equal two different fields. For a way between two places, require the actor's location to equal the way's first place field and the destination to equal its second, and name the reverse direction in limits.",
    "Checks and effects must use only allowed_state_paths.",
    "Do not invent source code, hidden state, or new entities.",
    "An action should change only state causally implied by its description and represented world.",
    "Use owner_ref expressions for actor ownership rather than hard-coded actor ids. {owner_ref: P} is the string 'actor:<id of P>', meaning 'owned by P'; it is NOT the owner of P. To require that thing X belongs to the acting actor, compare {participant: {name: X, path: ownership.owner_ref}} with {owner_ref: actor}. Never put {owner_ref: X} for a thing X on the left of a check (that would ask whether X owns itself).",
    "Terminal, if present, must be derivable from represented state and must not duplicate a completion flag.",
    "Limits and tests must name omissions and refusal/boundary cases honestly.",
    "Every check label is a short plain-English condition a visitor understands, using entity labels (e.g. \"the watering can is free\", \"Ava is holding the knife\"); never jargon such as actor, owner_ref, context, entity, component or participant.",
    "World kind: keeps running. terminal must be null. Add 1-4 processes so the world never runs down. If the description is about work or production: keep raw materials renewing at their source and demand arriving (raw materials regrow or are mined each round, customers want more, a served order becomes a new waiting order), so goods keep flowing through the chain and every actor role has something useful to do in most rounds, forever. A process never adds a good that an action makes from other goods (boards, parts, finished products): those come only from that action. A process never moves goods from one holder to another: carrying is an actor's action. A raw material has one source: if an actor's action gathers it (mining, cutting), that action takes it from a reserve and the process renews the reserve, not the gatherer's stock. Each actor's actions should be limited to its own step by checks on its role, and moving goods should add to the next holder's stock and subtract from the previous one's. If it is about people living their lives: change needs and resources every round in a cycle (for example hunger rises AND food regrows, supplies run down AND are restocked), never only decay, so they keep acting on their own needs with no goal imposed."
  ],
